<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tampere</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tammerfors</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tampere is a city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> on the Tammerkoski rapids between two large lakes. Founded in 1779, it used the water power of the rapids to become the country's leading industrial town, with textile mills such as Finlayson, and was a centre of the labour movement. In 1918 it was the main Red stronghold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finnish-civil-war">Finnish Civil War</a> until its capture by the Whites. It is now one of Finland's largest cities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tampere.jpg" alt="Tampere" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fenn-O-maniC · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tampere.lippu.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tampere lies in the Finnish Lakeland, between Lake Näsijärvi and Lake Pyhäjärvi, which differ in water level by 18 m. The Tammerkoski rapids that connect them have been an important source of power throughout history, most recently for generating electricity. The Pyynikki Ridge, a large esker formed from moraine during the Weichselian glaciation, rises 160 m above sea level. The name derives from the rapids, and the explanation most accepted traces it to a Proto-Samic phrase for a deep, slow part of a stream followed by the word for rapids.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest known permanent settlements around Tammerkoski date from the 7th century, when settlers from the west began to farm land in an area largely inhabited by Tavastian tribes. The population stayed small for centuries, and in 1638 Governor-General Per Brahe the Younger ordered two markets a year at Tammerkoski. The first industries in the 17th century were watermills and sawmills.</p><p>The neighbouring parish of Pirkkala had been the most important parish in the area through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. In 1771 and 1772 the pastor Erik Edner proposed a town on the banks of the rapids, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-iii">Gustav III of Sweden</a> made it a market town, and on 1 October 1779 Tampere received full town rights. Farming was forbidden within the city limits, so its inhabitants lived mainly by trade and crafts, and when the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> was formed in 1809 the town still had fewer than a thousand inhabitants.</p><p>Tampere became known as the Manchester of the North for its past as a centre of Finnish industry, and the red-brick factories along the Tammerkoski, such as Finlayson and Tampella, shaped the city. It was also the scene of political events in the early 20th century. In 1905 the conference of the Russian Social Democratic Labour Party, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a>, met in the Tampere Workers' Hall and decided to launch an armed insurrection. On 1 November 1905, during the General Strike, the Red Declaration was proclaimed in Keskustori.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tampere is the regional capital of Pirkanmaa and the most populous inland city in the Nordic countries, with about 263,000 inhabitants. Finnish is the first language of most of them. Its buildings range from the Old Stone Church of Messukylä, the only medieval one, to Tampere Cathedral. Pispala, a ridge between the two lakes, housed much of the industrial labour in the late 19th and early 20th centuries and joined the city in 1937.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tampere” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Reds controlled the cities and industrial centres of southern Finland, including Helsinki, <b>Tampere</b>, Turku and Viipuri.</blockquote><a href="/state/finnish-socialist-workers-republic">Finnish Socialist Workers' Republic</a></li></ul></section>
